WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:03.773
- Physical, and mental energy, to me,

00:00:03.773 --> 00:00:07.038
is the greatest riches of human life.

00:00:07.038 --> 00:00:08.374
Your blessing in life,

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:11.795
is when you find the torture
you're comfortable with.

00:00:11.795 --> 00:00:13.189
Comedians speaks to the public,

00:00:13.189 --> 00:00:15.672
in a way that no other person does.

00:00:15.672 --> 00:00:17.839
Privilege is toxic.
- Yeah.

00:00:19.156 --> 00:00:20.442
It really is.

00:00:20.442 --> 00:00:22.177
- He's an American comedian, actor,

00:00:22.177 --> 00:00:24.328
director, writer, and producer.

00:00:24.328 --> 00:00:26.815
He's best known, for portraying
a semi-fictional portrait

00:00:26.815 --> 00:00:28.984
of himself, in the sitcom, Seinfeld.

00:00:28.984 --> 00:00:31.301
In 2005, Comedy Central named him

00:00:31.301 --> 00:00:34.125
the 12th Greatest Standup
Comedian of All Time.

00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:35.188
He's Jerry Seinfeld,

00:00:35.188 --> 00:00:37.991
and here's my take on his
top ten rules of success.

00:00:37.991 --> 00:00:39.900
Rule number six is my personal favorite,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.640
and make sure to stick around,
all the way to the end,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.057
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:46.993 --> 00:00:51.321
- Putting in a phenomenal
day's work, to me,

00:00:51.321 --> 00:00:53.904
is way better than, you know...

00:00:56.334 --> 00:00:58.106
Let's say, you come into
a little chunk of money

00:00:58.106 --> 00:01:00.932
you didn't think you were going to get.

00:01:00.932 --> 00:01:02.118
You just get some dough,

00:01:02.118 --> 00:01:04.184
and you go hey, that's great.

00:01:04.184 --> 00:01:05.239
That is great.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.335
I think money's great, but energy,

00:01:08.335 --> 00:01:11.804
physical and mental energy, to me,

00:01:11.804 --> 00:01:15.447
is the greatest riches of human life.

00:01:15.447 --> 00:01:16.280
- [Howard] You can't look anything like--

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.780
- But, a lot of guys,
Howard, a lot of guys,

00:01:20.806 --> 00:01:22.478
they don't want to lift those weights.

00:01:22.478 --> 00:01:25.368
It's like goin' into the gym everyday.

00:01:25.368 --> 00:01:26.505
You know how you walk in everyday,

00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:28.768
and you go, oh geez, I
got to do this again.

00:01:28.768 --> 00:01:29.979
A lot of guys don't want to do it.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:30.812
It's brutal.

00:01:30.812 --> 00:01:32.008
- [Howard] It sounds like a tortured life,

00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:33.499
and you say it's not--

00:01:33.499 --> 00:01:34.639
- It is, it is.

00:01:34.639 --> 00:01:35.838
But, you know what?

00:01:35.838 --> 00:01:37.118
Your blessing in life,

00:01:37.118 --> 00:01:40.946
is when you find the torture
you're comfortable with.

00:01:40.946 --> 00:01:42.523
- [Howard] Well, wait a second.

00:01:42.523 --> 00:01:44.314
You have stated--

00:01:44.314 --> 00:01:45.858
- [Jerry] What, is that too rough, Robin?

00:01:45.858 --> 00:01:47.350
- [Howard] You want to go--

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.078
- Wait a minute, is that too rough?

00:01:49.078 --> 00:01:50.763
- [Robin] That was pretty profound!

00:01:50.763 --> 00:01:51.596
- Well, that's where I--

00:01:51.596 --> 00:01:53.308
- [Robin] Find the torture
you're comfortable with!

00:01:53.308 --> 00:01:54.301
That's life!

00:01:54.301 --> 00:01:58.468
- And that's marriage, it's
kids, it's work, it's exercise,

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.282
it's not eating the food you want to eat!

00:02:02.282 --> 00:02:04.562
Find the torture you're comfortable with,

00:02:04.562 --> 00:02:05.395
and you'll do well!

00:02:05.395 --> 00:02:06.533
- [Howard] I can't argue with that,

00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:09.255
because you know, even radio.

00:02:09.255 --> 00:02:11.542
I don't, it's torture for me.

00:02:11.542 --> 00:02:12.535
- [Jerry] Of course it is!

00:02:12.535 --> 00:02:15.109
- [Howard] But yet, I have to do it.

00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:15.942
- It's no different,

00:02:15.942 --> 00:02:18.821
than when you're 30
minutes into your workout.

00:02:18.821 --> 00:02:20.175
You're okay.

00:02:20.175 --> 00:02:21.737
And like you're okay now.

00:02:21.737 --> 00:02:23.132
You don't want to get your ass out of bed.

00:02:23.132 --> 00:02:25.522
You don't want to sit in
that awful chair again.

00:02:25.522 --> 00:02:28.787
But, once you get goin', and that's life.

00:02:28.787 --> 00:02:31.193
If you master that, you've mastered life.

00:02:31.193 --> 00:02:34.749
What I am good at, and what
every comedian is good at,

00:02:34.749 --> 00:02:37.582
is knowing how to carefully manage

00:02:39.365 --> 00:02:41.208
an audience's attention span.

00:02:41.208 --> 00:02:43.980
And that is also the key to this show.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.979
For example, I'll give you another,

00:02:45.979 --> 00:02:49.908
when this show was first cut together,

00:02:49.908 --> 00:02:52.269
again, and go back to that
little scene with the phone,

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:55.145
about net-a-porter,
just talkin' to my wife.

00:02:55.145 --> 00:02:57.387
And Julia says, "Ask your wife about,

00:02:57.387 --> 00:02:58.848
"this thing about net-a-porter."

00:02:58.848 --> 00:03:01.513
I go, you know what, I'm
going to ask her right now.

00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:04.832
And I take the phone,
and I dial the phone,

00:03:04.832 --> 00:03:06.956
and then, Jess picks
up the phone, and I go,

00:03:06.956 --> 00:03:09.177
hi Jess, I'm sitting here with Julia.

00:03:09.177 --> 00:03:10.010
I'm in the middle,

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.040
we're actually right in the
middle of doing an episode.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.076
She has a question, about net-a-porter.

00:03:15.076 --> 00:03:18.219
Do you mind, can I ask you
right now, are you busy?

00:03:18.219 --> 00:03:21.837
Okay, you got to get rid of all of that.

00:03:21.837 --> 00:03:25.420
All of that goes,
because it's just boring.

00:03:27.254 --> 00:03:31.909
Now, it's not boring,
in a TV series, you can,

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:33.407
when you're watching a TV series,

00:03:33.407 --> 00:03:34.609
you're watching an episode of a sitcom,

00:03:34.609 --> 00:03:36.941
there's a story that you're hooked on,

00:03:36.941 --> 00:03:39.090
and there are characters.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.697
And this is going somewhere.

00:03:40.697 --> 00:03:44.124
This thing is going nowhere.
(audience laughing)

00:03:44.124 --> 00:03:44.957
So...

00:03:46.413 --> 00:03:48.280
Even when they first cut it together,

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:49.472
they even had me dialing.

00:03:49.472 --> 00:03:50.979
No, no, no, you can't have that.

00:03:50.979 --> 00:03:53.979
That microsecond is on the Internet,

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.475
'cause the Internet is
the most brutal medium

00:03:58.475 --> 00:03:59.832
for attention span.

00:03:59.832 --> 00:04:03.999
The distance, from your
finger, to that off, or stop,

00:04:05.695 --> 00:04:07.389
you're holding it right there,

00:04:07.389 --> 00:04:09.153
while you're watching the thing.

00:04:09.153 --> 00:04:11.619
How many times, you see
a video, and you go,

00:04:11.619 --> 00:04:13.588
oh that looks good, I want to see that.

00:04:13.588 --> 00:04:15.273
You press it then, but
15 second commercial.

00:04:15.273 --> 00:04:16.962
You're like, no, no, no, no, no!

00:04:16.962 --> 00:04:19.318
(audience laughing)
No, you don't get 15 seconds.

00:04:19.318 --> 00:04:20.901
It's not that good.

00:04:22.058 --> 00:04:23.905
Like Amazon.com, one click ordering.

00:04:23.905 --> 00:04:25.151
That's where I got this idea.

00:04:25.151 --> 00:04:27.255
They've understood, two clicks,

00:04:27.255 --> 00:04:30.482
I don't even want it anymore!
(audience laughing)

00:04:30.482 --> 00:04:33.565
I changed my mind, in between clicks!

00:04:37.722 --> 00:04:40.163
As a standup comedian,

00:04:40.163 --> 00:04:44.330
you must master that attention
span dynamic, of an audience.

00:04:47.338 --> 00:04:49.924
And then, it's no different from

00:04:49.924 --> 00:04:52.180
trying to do drugs, with
four drunks in a nightclub,

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.762
at two o'clock in the morning.

00:04:53.762 --> 00:04:55.968
You've got to get their
attention, and hold it,

00:04:55.968 --> 00:04:57.912
so you spend your life doing it,

00:04:57.912 --> 00:05:02.828
and that's what you have to do,
on the Internet, to survive.

00:05:02.828 --> 00:05:04.749
Comedians speaks to the public in a way,

00:05:04.749 --> 00:05:07.215
that no other person does.

00:05:07.215 --> 00:05:10.163
He talks about things, that we all say,

00:05:10.163 --> 00:05:14.263
commercials and things, and it's special.

00:05:14.263 --> 00:05:17.306
It's a unique podium,
being a standup comic.

00:05:17.306 --> 00:05:18.721
You have free range,

00:05:18.721 --> 00:05:20.954
and you don't even really
have to make a point,

00:05:20.954 --> 00:05:24.707
but you have to relate to
people on a very personal level,

00:05:24.707 --> 00:05:27.491
and I was afraid, I'm still
afraid of losing that,

00:05:27.491 --> 00:05:31.074
'cause I think that's
the key to my own sanity,

00:05:31.074 --> 00:05:34.164
as well as my continued success.

00:05:34.164 --> 00:05:37.073
- If you could go back
to being younger Jerry,

00:05:37.073 --> 00:05:38.360
would you go back there?

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.920
I mean, you know...

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.595
- Would I want to be young again?

00:05:41.595 --> 00:05:42.428
No.

00:05:42.428 --> 00:05:43.337
- [Howard] You're happy where you are.

00:05:43.337 --> 00:05:47.213
- I think, if you're a
little lucky in life,

00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:49.851
you should enjoy getting older.

00:05:49.851 --> 00:05:50.684
- [Howard] Right.

00:05:50.684 --> 00:05:53.405
- Because, you're going to see more.

00:05:53.405 --> 00:05:56.698
When you're young, you can't
see what's goin' on so well.

00:05:56.698 --> 00:05:59.563
You get older, you walk into
a room, you meet this guy,

00:05:59.563 --> 00:06:00.396
and that guy,

00:06:00.396 --> 00:06:02.231
you go, oh I see exactly
what's going on here.

00:06:02.231 --> 00:06:03.303
I love that.

00:06:03.303 --> 00:06:04.952
- And don't you kind of like--
- And I don't care about

00:06:04.952 --> 00:06:06.838
the physical deterioration.

00:06:06.838 --> 00:06:07.671
- [Howard] You don't?

00:06:07.671 --> 00:06:08.879
- No.

00:06:08.879 --> 00:06:10.060
That's part of it.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:14.111
It's a nice feeling, to invent something,

00:06:14.111 --> 00:06:17.888
where there was no
template there, before you,

00:06:17.888 --> 00:06:19.210
to figure it out.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:21.925
I mean, I remember, I
told the idea to Louis CK,

00:06:21.925 --> 00:06:24.196
and I said, I have this idea of,

00:06:24.196 --> 00:06:26.719
I'll hang out with
comedians, and I'll shoot it,

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:29.039
and I'll cut it together,
with music and coffee,

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:31.220
and I said, what do I do with that?

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.371
And he says, "You should charge
people a dollar, to watch.

00:06:35.371 --> 00:06:38.150
"Charge everyone a dollar," you know.

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:39.639
And I didn't like that.

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:41.001
Nobody knew what to do.

00:06:41.001 --> 00:06:44.314
I went, I had this big meeting at CAA,

00:06:44.314 --> 00:06:47.897
where there's really
smart, digital people.

00:06:49.931 --> 00:06:51.412
There was like ten people in the room,

00:06:51.412 --> 00:06:53.083
people I had read about, never met!

00:06:53.083 --> 00:06:56.129
This is the guy, who meets
with Zuckerberg everyday!

00:06:56.129 --> 00:06:57.598
(audience laughing)

00:06:57.598 --> 00:06:59.431
This is way out there!

00:07:02.135 --> 00:07:04.623
And I go, okay, well I got this idea,

00:07:04.623 --> 00:07:08.623
and it seems like, as a
person that people know,

00:07:10.168 --> 00:07:11.077
I have an audience.

00:07:11.077 --> 00:07:13.082
People know me.

00:07:13.082 --> 00:07:15.051
So, we can get people to check it out,

00:07:15.051 --> 00:07:18.687
and the Internet seems
like a pretty fluid medium.

00:07:18.687 --> 00:07:22.550
I could put this thing on the Internet.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:25.786
What do you think I could
do, and they went...

00:07:25.786 --> 00:07:28.126
(audience laughing)

00:07:28.126 --> 00:07:28.959
No idea.

00:07:31.209 --> 00:07:33.836
And then, I met with
these Facebook people,

00:07:33.836 --> 00:07:36.336
and I met with YouTube people.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.759
A guy like me, that's one of
the fun things about being me,

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:42.700
is you can meet anybody.
(audience laughing)

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:45.028
You can call anybody, and
say, can I talk to you,

00:07:45.028 --> 00:07:47.695
and they go, "Sure, come on in!"

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:50.568
And they're all smart!

00:07:50.568 --> 00:07:53.047
And they were nice, and
they were well-intentioned,

00:07:53.047 --> 00:07:57.218
and they all tell me, this will not work!

00:07:57.218 --> 00:07:59.271
They said, if you're over five minutes,

00:07:59.271 --> 00:08:01.866
nobody's going to, they're
not going to hang in there,

00:08:01.866 --> 00:08:02.792
'cause the Internet is,

00:08:02.792 --> 00:08:05.250
five minutes is the
maximum length of time,

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:07.916
and we average 19 minutes a viewer.

00:08:07.916 --> 00:08:10.300
So, that was pretty cool!

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.248
I think the breakthrough is when

00:08:12.248 --> 00:08:15.755
people are talking about
your act, to other people,

00:08:15.755 --> 00:08:18.265
and they can't do it,
you know what I mean?

00:08:18.265 --> 00:08:21.296
They start to do it, and
they go, I can't do it,

00:08:21.296 --> 00:08:22.939
it's the way he does it,

00:08:22.939 --> 00:08:25.541
that it's so ingrained
in your personality,

00:08:25.541 --> 00:08:27.859
and the way you talk,
and the way you behave,

00:08:27.859 --> 00:08:29.513
that people cannot imitate it.

00:08:29.513 --> 00:08:31.722
- [Interviewer] How did you find it?

00:08:31.722 --> 00:08:33.562
- You just...

00:08:33.562 --> 00:08:34.996
- I mean, did you sit there, and say--

00:08:34.996 --> 00:08:36.275
- The audience teaches you--

00:08:36.275 --> 00:08:37.108
- The audience does?

00:08:37.108 --> 00:08:38.842
- The audience teaches you over the years.

00:08:38.842 --> 00:08:39.885
That's the way I learned.

00:08:39.885 --> 00:08:42.938
You just, night after night,
show after show, after show,

00:08:42.938 --> 00:08:44.958
you're doing something,
and they laugh, and you go,

00:08:44.958 --> 00:08:46.462
why did they laugh at that?

00:08:46.462 --> 00:08:48.165
And but, the next night, you
do it again the same way,

00:08:48.165 --> 00:08:50.281
and if they laugh, then you build on it.

00:08:50.281 --> 00:08:52.868
- [Robin] You walk into a place, you know,

00:08:52.868 --> 00:08:54.744
that everybody says, that's Seinfeld.

00:08:54.744 --> 00:08:56.215
- No, I don't think like that.

00:08:56.215 --> 00:08:57.048
- [Robin] You don't?

00:08:57.048 --> 00:08:58.819
- No, I'm so lost in my own head,

00:08:58.819 --> 00:09:00.386
I'm not thinkin' about what other,

00:09:00.386 --> 00:09:04.141
people think, do people expect
you to be funny all the time?

00:09:04.141 --> 00:09:05.291
I get that question.

00:09:05.291 --> 00:09:07.056
What do I know, what people expect,

00:09:07.056 --> 00:09:10.251
nor what do I care what people expect?

00:09:10.251 --> 00:09:12.313
Your expectations are your problem!

00:09:12.313 --> 00:09:14.783
- [Robin] You don't have
to perform for people!

00:09:14.783 --> 00:09:18.148
- I try to, because I
like to, but I don't care!

00:09:18.148 --> 00:09:20.315
Privilege is toxic.
- Yeah.

00:09:21.655 --> 00:09:22.633
It really is.

00:09:22.633 --> 00:09:24.852
- Things that people struggle to achieve,

00:09:24.852 --> 00:09:27.228
they get to positions
with power, influence.

00:09:27.228 --> 00:09:28.552
Money can do things.

00:09:28.552 --> 00:09:31.274
It has a toxic effect on their judgment.

00:09:31.274 --> 00:09:32.410
- Yes.

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:33.647
Has that happened to you, yet?

00:09:33.647 --> 00:09:34.480
- No.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:35.972
- Why is that, do you think.

00:09:35.972 --> 00:09:39.500
(both talking at once)

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:42.645
So, you're a funny guy.
- Thank you.

00:09:42.645 --> 00:09:45.022
- You've gone on some
late night talk shows,

00:09:45.022 --> 00:09:47.500
people think you're funny,
they give you a show...

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:48.333
- Right.

00:09:48.333 --> 00:09:49.836
- [Barack] And then, next thing you know,

00:09:49.836 --> 00:09:51.825
you've made a ridiculous amount of money.

00:09:51.825 --> 00:09:53.793
- So much more than you.

00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:55.822
And yeah, it's...

00:09:55.822 --> 00:09:56.768
How do I seem to you?

00:09:56.768 --> 00:09:59.201
Do I seem spoiled, out of touch?

00:09:59.201 --> 00:10:00.478
- You know, I don't know.

00:10:00.478 --> 00:10:02.158
- You have a pretty good
instinct for people.

00:10:02.158 --> 00:10:04.385
- Right now, you seem like
a completely normal guy.

00:10:04.385 --> 00:10:05.914
- But, I'm putting on an act,

00:10:05.914 --> 00:10:07.223
like everyone else does for you.

00:10:07.223 --> 00:10:08.765
- That's my point.

00:10:08.765 --> 00:10:10.395
That's what I'm gettin' at.

00:10:10.395 --> 00:10:12.830
But, I'm going to probe this.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:16.997
The question is, how did you
calibrate dealing with that?

00:10:19.774 --> 00:10:21.944
Now, at a certain point, you
might've thought to yourself,

00:10:21.944 --> 00:10:24.476
you know what, I'm more
than just a comedian.

00:10:24.476 --> 00:10:28.138
I'm going to make a Jewish
version of Citizen Kane.

00:10:28.138 --> 00:10:29.836
You know, how did you keep perspective?

00:10:29.836 --> 00:10:31.706
- [Jerry] I'll give you the real answer.

00:10:31.706 --> 00:10:33.743
It's got to be similar to your life.

00:10:33.743 --> 00:10:35.483
I fell in love with the work.

00:10:35.483 --> 00:10:40.244
And the work was joyful, and
difficult, and interesting,

00:10:40.244 --> 00:10:42.566
and that was my focus.

00:10:42.566 --> 00:10:44.231
Food is so complicated as an adult.

00:10:44.231 --> 00:10:45.980
It's just, where you see
people in the supermarket,

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:47.710
they're just sweatin'.

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:50.861
Nobody knows, what do I
eat, the protein, the carbs,

00:10:50.861 --> 00:10:52.398
the fat content!

00:10:52.398 --> 00:10:55.075
Oh, my God, fat content!

00:10:55.075 --> 00:10:56.085
You're just walkin' up to each other,

00:10:56.085 --> 00:10:57.354
you look pretty good, what are you eating?

00:10:57.354 --> 00:11:00.673
Maybe, I'll eat that.
(audience laughing)

00:11:00.673 --> 00:11:02.501
The whole supermarket itself is designed

00:11:02.501 --> 00:11:05.001
to break down your
sense of having any life

00:11:05.001 --> 00:11:06.616
outside the supermarket.

00:11:06.616 --> 00:11:07.473
It's like a casino.

00:11:07.473 --> 00:11:11.587
There's no clocks, no windows,
no easily accessible exits.

00:11:11.587 --> 00:11:13.174
You ever not buy anything
in the supermarket,

00:11:13.174 --> 00:11:14.100
and try and get out of there?

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:15.562
It's impossible.

00:11:15.562 --> 00:11:17.145
There's no way out.

00:11:18.801 --> 00:11:19.663
You can see what happens to people.

00:11:19.663 --> 00:11:21.076
When they're walking
up to the supermarket,

00:11:21.076 --> 00:11:22.428
they really have a whole sense of purpose.

00:11:22.428 --> 00:11:23.963
I'm going to get this,
I'm going to get that.

00:11:23.963 --> 00:11:25.344
I'm going to pay for it, I'm
going to get out of here,

00:11:25.344 --> 00:11:27.075
and get right back to my normal life.

00:11:27.075 --> 00:11:29.447
You see that same person,
ten minutes later, just...

00:11:29.447 --> 00:11:32.447
(audience laughing)

00:11:41.161 --> 00:11:42.744
What aisle is this?

00:11:44.144 --> 00:11:46.720
Why did I come up here?

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.069
They're just noticing something new.

00:11:49.069 --> 00:11:52.486
Oh, they got 'em in mesquite flavor, now.

00:11:53.582 --> 00:11:54.999
What is mesquite?

00:11:56.513 --> 00:11:59.680
I wonder if it's made from mosquitoes.

00:12:03.642 --> 00:12:05.437
Produce section, I always
find very challenging.

00:12:05.437 --> 00:12:08.229
There's always some special
thing you're supposed to know,

00:12:08.229 --> 00:12:09.973
you know, about each fruit.

00:12:09.973 --> 00:12:11.672
Summertime, make sure your peaches,

00:12:11.672 --> 00:12:14.249
or I don't know, whatever it is.

00:12:14.249 --> 00:12:15.100
You got to fake it, you know,

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:18.091
I'm shakin' stuff, holdin'
it up to the light.

00:12:18.091 --> 00:12:20.916
Yeah, that's a good one!

00:12:20.916 --> 00:12:23.166
Sure glad I found that one!

00:12:25.523 --> 00:12:28.428
Cantaloupe's rollin' down the aisle.

00:12:28.428 --> 00:12:32.004
See where that's floating
left, that was not ready.

00:12:32.004 --> 00:12:33.837
I don't want that one.

00:12:35.442 --> 00:12:38.308
I'm very impressed with this
seedless watermelon product

00:12:38.308 --> 00:12:39.816
that they have for us.

00:12:39.816 --> 00:12:41.056
They've done it.

00:12:41.056 --> 00:12:43.518
We now have seedless watermelon.

00:12:43.518 --> 00:12:44.351
Pretty amazing.

00:12:44.351 --> 00:12:47.108
What are they planting, to
grow this seedless watermelon,

00:12:47.108 --> 00:12:47.941
I wonder.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.167
The melons aren't humpin', are they?

00:12:50.167 --> 00:12:52.762
They must be plantin' something.

00:12:52.762 --> 00:12:54.345
How does this work?

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:58.189
And what kind of scientists
do this type of work?

00:12:58.189 --> 00:13:01.393
I read I thing, it was
15 years in development.

00:13:01.393 --> 00:13:03.992
In the laboratories, with gene splicing,

00:13:03.992 --> 00:13:06.164
or whatever they do there, I don't know.

00:13:06.164 --> 00:13:07.906
I mean, other scientists,
workin' on AIDS, cancer,

00:13:07.906 --> 00:13:08.739
heart disease,

00:13:08.739 --> 00:13:12.898
these guys are going, no, I'm
going to devote myself to melon.

00:13:12.898 --> 00:13:15.372
I think that's much more important.

00:13:15.372 --> 00:13:17.253
Sure, thousands are dying needlessly,

00:13:17.253 --> 00:13:19.194
but this, (thump noise)
that's got to stop.

00:13:19.194 --> 00:13:23.277
(audience laughing and clapping)

00:13:26.128 --> 00:13:28.135
Have you ever try, and pick
up a wet one off the floor?

00:13:28.135 --> 00:13:30.514
It's almost impossible.

00:13:30.514 --> 00:13:34.681
I really think we should devote
the money to these studies.

00:13:36.065 --> 00:13:37.847
Milk is a big problem for
people in the supermarket.

00:13:37.847 --> 00:13:39.020
They're never quite sure if they have it,

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:39.853
or if they need it.

00:13:39.853 --> 00:13:41.665
They bury it, way in the
back, in the supermarket.

00:13:41.665 --> 00:13:43.099
You got to find it, you
got to hack your way

00:13:43.099 --> 00:13:45.098
through all the displays.

00:13:45.098 --> 00:13:46.520
There it is!

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:48.099
There's the milk!

00:13:48.099 --> 00:13:49.891
Do we have any milk?

00:13:49.891 --> 00:13:52.589
People are never really
sure if they have milk.

00:13:52.589 --> 00:13:55.423
You think you have milk,
we might have milk.

00:13:55.423 --> 00:13:56.364
I know there's a carton in there.

00:13:56.364 --> 00:13:59.043
I don't know how much is in it.

00:13:59.043 --> 00:14:01.770
Well, what should we do?

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:03.362
'Cause, you want to be sure.

00:14:03.362 --> 00:14:04.806
There's nothing worse, than
thinking you have milk,

00:14:04.806 --> 00:14:06.301
and not having it.

00:14:06.301 --> 00:14:07.719
You know, you got the
bowl set up, the cereal,

00:14:07.719 --> 00:14:09.670
the spoon, the napkin,
the TV, the newspaper,

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:11.346
everything's ready to go.

00:14:11.346 --> 00:14:13.431
You got to lift up the carton,
and it's too light. (exclaims)

00:14:13.431 --> 00:14:18.239
(audience laughing and clapping)

00:14:18.239 --> 00:14:19.072
Oh, no!

00:14:21.364 --> 00:14:22.197
Too light!

00:14:24.071 --> 00:14:24.904
Or sometimes, you think you need,

00:14:24.904 --> 00:14:27.464
but hey, we better pick up some milk.

00:14:27.464 --> 00:14:30.449
Like, many of you are thinking right now.

00:14:30.449 --> 00:14:33.932
You know, he's right, maybe
we should pick up some milk.

00:14:33.932 --> 00:14:36.392
So, you pick up some milk on the way home,

00:14:36.392 --> 00:14:38.970
and then you'll discover,
you already have milk.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.108
And now, you got way too much milk.

00:14:41.108 --> 00:14:41.941
That's no good either.

00:14:41.941 --> 00:14:42.969
Now, it's a race against the clock,

00:14:42.969 --> 00:14:45.800
with the expiration date.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.492
That freaky thought.

00:14:47.492 --> 00:14:49.155
Now, you're eatin' giant
punch bowls of cereal,

00:14:49.155 --> 00:14:51.442
three meals a day.

00:14:51.442 --> 00:14:54.145
You're washing your face with milk.

00:14:54.145 --> 00:14:55.862
Bringin' cats in, from
all over the neighborhood,

00:14:55.862 --> 00:14:57.835
hurry up and drink it!

00:14:57.835 --> 00:15:00.002
Come on, it's almost time!

00:15:02.166 --> 00:15:03.749
Get back over here.

00:15:07.324 --> 00:15:11.239
How do they know, that that
is the definite exact day?

00:15:11.239 --> 00:15:13.277
You know, they don't say,
like it's in a vicinity,

00:15:13.277 --> 00:15:15.110
give or take, roughly.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:18.004
They brand it, right into
the side of the carton,

00:15:18.004 --> 00:15:21.920
and then they... (sizzle noise)

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:24.920
That's your goddamn day right there!

00:15:25.855 --> 00:15:28.315
Oh, don't screw with us.

00:15:28.315 --> 00:15:31.866
We know what day is the final day!

00:15:31.866 --> 00:15:33.866
And then, it is so over!

00:15:36.305 --> 00:15:38.416
You ever have milk, the day after the day?

00:15:38.416 --> 00:15:39.595
Scares the hell out of you, doesn't it?

00:15:39.595 --> 00:15:44.074
(audience laughing and clapping)

00:15:44.074 --> 00:15:46.796
The spoon is trembling, as
it comes out of the bowl.

00:15:46.796 --> 00:15:48.942
It's after the day!

00:15:48.942 --> 00:15:51.625
I'm takin' a big chance!

00:15:51.625 --> 00:15:52.686
I smell it, you smell it,

00:15:52.686 --> 00:15:55.519
what is it supposed to smell like?

00:15:57.013 --> 00:15:59.180
It smells like milk to me!

00:16:02.553 --> 00:16:05.262
I don't know how they're
so definite, though.

00:16:05.262 --> 00:16:08.981
Maybe the cows tip 'em off,
when they're milking 'em.

00:16:08.981 --> 00:16:10.121
July third.

00:16:10.121 --> 00:16:14.204
(audience laughing and clapping)

00:16:21.974 --> 00:16:23.177
- Thank you guys so much for watching!

00:16:23.177 --> 00:16:26.092
I made this video, because
kennymean asked me to.

00:16:26.092 --> 00:16:27.275
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:16:27.275 --> 00:16:28.897
that you want me to profile next,

00:16:28.897 --> 00:16:30.974
leave it in the comments below,
and I'll see what I can do.

00:16:30.974 --> 00:16:31.807
I'd also love to know which

00:16:31.807 --> 00:16:33.324
of Jerry Seinfeld's top ten rules

00:16:33.324 --> 00:16:35.021
had the biggest impact on you, and why,

00:16:35.021 --> 00:16:36.979
what change are you going
to make, in your business,

00:16:36.979 --> 00:16:38.553
as a result of watching this video?

00:16:38.553 --> 00:16:40.227
What clip is your favorite?

00:16:40.227 --> 00:16:41.060
Leave it in the comments,

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:42.548
and I will join in the discussion.

00:16:42.548 --> 00:16:44.963
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout out to Dre Baldwin,

00:16:44.963 --> 00:16:47.269
from DreAllDay.com.

00:16:47.269 --> 00:16:49.009
Thank you, Dre, for picking
up a copy of my book.

00:16:49.009 --> 00:16:49.842
If you guys watching,

00:16:49.842 --> 00:16:51.935
if you want to chance at a
shout out, in a future video,

00:16:51.935 --> 00:16:53.252
make sure to grab the book,

00:16:53.252 --> 00:16:55.399
and email in your receipts,
so we can keep track.

00:16:55.399 --> 00:16:57.059
Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:16:57.059 --> 00:17:00.256
continue to believe, or
whatever your one word is,

00:17:00.256 --> 00:17:02.089
and I'll see you soon.

00:17:07.643 --> 00:17:09.117
- So, with Letterman retiring,

00:17:09.117 --> 00:17:12.118
that's another one of your
peers that was retiring,

00:17:12.118 --> 00:17:14.220
and I know, you said, you'll
never stop doing standup,

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:17.937
but I was wondering, what
drives you to do this show?

00:17:17.937 --> 00:17:18.950
The things outside standup.

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:22.915
Like, what is bringing you to do that?

00:17:22.915 --> 00:17:25.164
- I did this show, really first of all,

00:17:25.164 --> 00:17:26.349
I never thought it was a show.

00:17:26.349 --> 00:17:28.956
It was very actually, just very recently,

00:17:28.956 --> 00:17:29.861
that I started to think,

00:17:29.861 --> 00:17:32.634
maybe this is actually something!

00:17:32.634 --> 00:17:34.658
(Jerry and audience laughing)

00:17:34.658 --> 00:17:37.741
But, I kind of missed, from talk shows,

00:17:39.156 --> 00:17:43.199
the funny talking of casually hanging out.

00:17:43.199 --> 00:17:46.398
There's no casual hanging
out, anymore on talk shows.

00:17:46.398 --> 00:17:49.226
The real estate is too valuable,

00:17:49.226 --> 00:17:50.644
and people want to push their product,

00:17:50.644 --> 00:17:54.052
and I don't know, so I
came up with this idea,

00:17:54.052 --> 00:17:55.235
of like, if I ever did a talk,

00:17:55.235 --> 00:17:56.458
see the reason I never did a talk show,

00:17:56.458 --> 00:17:58.416
is 'cause I can't really talk to people

00:17:58.416 --> 00:18:00.774
that aren't comedians, and be funny.

00:18:00.774 --> 00:18:01.607
It's hard.

00:18:02.449 --> 00:18:03.293
You're doing just great, though. (laughs)

00:18:03.293 --> 00:18:07.376
(audience laughing and clapping)

00:18:09.369 --> 00:18:10.780
- It's only been for three minutes.

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:12.526
- Yeah, yeah.

00:18:12.526 --> 00:18:16.945
So, I don't know, this was
my personal fantasy, idea,

00:18:16.945 --> 00:18:18.817
of what I would like to watch.

00:18:18.817 --> 00:18:20.356
And standup is becoming so popular

00:18:20.356 --> 00:18:22.628
with some of the young people now.

00:18:22.628 --> 00:18:25.277
There's so many standup geeks, you know.

00:18:25.277 --> 00:18:26.834
They would like this.

00:18:26.834 --> 00:18:28.590
So, I just made a few of them,

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:29.696
and just threw 'em on the Internet.

00:18:29.696 --> 00:18:32.924
We didn't do any press,
or sell it in any way,

00:18:32.924 --> 00:18:34.893
and you know, that's just so cool.

00:18:34.893 --> 00:18:37.660
The Internet fascinated
me, also, as a network.

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:40.868
I thought, why do I need a TV network?

00:18:40.868 --> 00:18:42.287
This is a network.

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:44.148
So, that was what drew me to it.

00:18:44.148 --> 00:18:45.730
- [Howard] Do you ever dream of the day,

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:47.885
where you could go, with your wife,

00:18:47.885 --> 00:18:50.010
to a Chinese restaurant, and sit there,

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:52.411
and not think about, that the
chopsticks are ridiculous--

00:18:52.411 --> 00:18:54.481
- I'd shoot myself in
the mouth, with a bullet.

00:18:54.481 --> 00:18:55.471
- [Howard] Would you?

00:18:55.471 --> 00:18:58.887
- What fun is life, if I'm
not making jokes all the time?

00:18:58.887 --> 00:19:01.346
- [Howard] But, you said,
it's a torturer, also.

00:19:01.346 --> 00:19:02.966
- It's a torture I love!

00:19:02.966 --> 00:19:06.106
- [Howard] Wow, you really know yourself.

00:19:06.106 --> 00:19:08.750
- I guess I do, I guess I do.

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:12.525
You want to know the key to
comedy, right now, in two words?

00:19:12.525 --> 00:19:15.064
'Cause, you guys are going
to be doing more comedy bits,

00:19:15.064 --> 00:19:15.897
on this show?

00:19:15.897 --> 00:19:18.094
Let me explain the basic thing,

00:19:18.094 --> 00:19:20.348
really, all you need to know.

00:19:20.348 --> 00:19:21.515
Loud and fast.

00:19:23.665 --> 00:19:27.047
Do not leave pauses, between the lines.

00:19:27.047 --> 00:19:31.443
Say it loud, say it fast,
and you're 80 percent there.

00:19:31.443 --> 00:19:32.794
When people would come on the show,

00:19:32.794 --> 00:19:34.983
actors, sometimes good actors,

00:19:34.983 --> 00:19:36.506
they would come on, to do a scene,

00:19:36.506 --> 00:19:38.863
or do a part on the show.

00:19:38.863 --> 00:19:40.336
That's all we would tell 'em.

00:19:40.336 --> 00:19:42.919
Louder, faster, louder, faster.

00:19:44.747 --> 00:19:47.613
You can't fix, the material
is the material, right?

00:19:47.613 --> 00:19:49.359
You're going to do it.

00:19:49.359 --> 00:19:51.662
That was pretty good,
that was pretty good.

00:19:51.662 --> 00:19:54.455
- I thought he was going
to shish kebab you guys.

00:19:54.455 --> 00:19:58.447
- No, I would say, I would
make it 30 seconds shorter...

00:19:58.447 --> 00:20:00.107
(group laughing)

00:20:00.107 --> 00:20:02.746
And I would pull out some of the air.

00:20:02.746 --> 00:20:05.280
Do you edit that, or is that just...

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:07.054
You do edit it, a little bit, okay.

00:20:07.054 --> 00:20:09.462
So, I would have just pulled
out a little more air,

00:20:09.462 --> 00:20:10.927
and I thought it played pretty well.

00:20:10.927 --> 00:20:11.842
- [Dan] The best...

00:20:11.842 --> 00:20:14.196
Who captured a character, out of these--

00:20:14.196 --> 00:20:15.985
- They were all good.

00:20:15.985 --> 00:20:17.205
You were good.

00:20:17.205 --> 00:20:19.493
- Fritzy, as George?
- He was good.

00:20:19.493 --> 00:20:21.736
You know why, he talked quickly.

00:20:21.736 --> 00:20:22.569
- [Dan] He always does.

00:20:22.569 --> 00:20:23.402
What about Paulie?

00:20:23.402 --> 00:20:24.865
- [Jerry] In comedy,
you got to talk quickly,

00:20:24.865 --> 00:20:27.937
because you want to get the mind goin'.

